                                     OS9DSK                                                        copyright (c) 1997 by Bob Devries                                              may be freely distributes                                   OS9DSK is a programme to read the  directories  of,  and copy files     from Jeff Vavasour's emulator OS9 .DSK files.  That is, if you  have a     disk image file created by Jeff's emulator,  that  is  an  image of an     OS-9 disk, OS9DSK will work with it.             So what does it do?             OS9DSK -h        OS9DSK -?        OS9DSK -help             All these produce the help message:             Usage:   OS9DSK -dir filename.DSK DSKpath                 OS9DSK -get filename.DSK DSKpath os9file                 OS9DSK -proc filename.DSK DSKpath             Here's an explanation of each:             OS9DSK -dir filename.DSK DSKpath             This will  do a directory of the emulator .DSK file 'filename.DSK',     just as if you had typed: 'chd /d0;dir'. The DSKpath is  optional, and     if omitted, the root directory is shown.             EXAMPLE:             OS9DSK -dir /h1/DSK/pubdom19.dsk              0.0 85/11/29 2050   d-ewrewr      A       608   CMDS         0.0 85/11/29 2053   d-ewrewr     13       128   BASIC09         0.0 85/11/29 2054   d-ewrewr     1C        96   LIB         0.0 85/11/29 2054   d-ewrewr     25       128   DEFS         0.0 85/11/29 2055   d-ewrewr     2E       224   C         0.0 00/00/00 0000   d-ewrewr    10A       160   ASM             OS9DSK ALWAYS does extended  directories!  That  way you can see at     first glance which are files, and which are directories.             OS9DSK -get filename.DSK DSKpath os9file              This will copy a file from the path DSKpath to the your OS-9 disk,     just   as  if   you'd   typed:   'copy   /d0/DSKpath   os9file'.   The     'filename.DSK'  must  be  the  complete pathname to the emulator file,     and the DSKpath must be the complete pathname to the wanted file.             EXAMPLE:             OS9DSK -get /h1/DSK/pubdom19.dsk CMDS/crypt /r0/crypt              This will copy the file 'crypt' from the  CMDS  directory  of  the     emulator file to your ramdisk.             OS9DSK -proc filename.DSK DSKpath             This will produce a procedure file (like  DSAVE)  with the commands     necessary  to  copy  the  complete  file  structure from the  supplied     DSKpath down, or, if DSKpath is omitted, from the root directory.             EXAMPLE:             OS9DSK -proc /h1/DSK/pubdom20.dsk             -nx        -np        tmode -w=1 nopause        makdir CMDS        chd CMDS        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk CMDS/advent advent        chd ..        makdir MISC        chd MISC        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent.doc advent.doc        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent1.dat advent1.dat        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent2.dat advent2.dat        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent3.dat advent3.dat        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent4.dat advent4.dat        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent5.dat advent5.dat        os9dsk -get /h1/dsk/pubdom20.dsk MISC/advent6.dat advent6.dat        chd ..        -n        -p        tmode -w=1 pause             So you can output this to a file for editing purposes, like this:             OS9DSK -proc filename.DSK >procedure.file             or, you can pipe it to a shell, like this:             OS9DSK -proc filename.DSK ! shell             This way all  the  filenames  on  the  emulator  .DSK  file will be     copied to your current directory.                              ______________________________                                  This  is  the  first 'cut' of this programme, so it may still have     some bugs. If so, please let me know.  At  this  stage,  I  have  only     tested this on an MM/1  computer,  with  a  few  .DSK  files.  It will     require some fairly extensive rewriting to make it compile on a COCO.             I am reachable by email at: bdevries@gil.com.au        or by regular mail at:             Bob Devries        21 Virgo Street        INALA, Qld, 4077        AUSTRALIA             PH: +61 7 32787209             Date: Monday, 31st March, 1997